      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. It's a decent start, but I think it's still a bit too surface-level for a senior reviewer at Oxford. It reads a little bit like a summary rather than a deep, critical dive.\n\nI need you to really lean into the \"Critical Comments\" aspect. I need you to go through the methodology and find the actual cracks. For example, focus heavily on why their claim about long-range dependencies might be a fallacy, or where their experimental setup is actually insufficient. \n\nIf you can make the critique more pointed and use more precise critical language to describe the flaws you're finding, that would be better. Again, I'm watching out for filler, so make sure every critique of an \"oversight\" or \"limitation\" is actually backed by the paper's methodology, but I do need the tone to be much more assertive and critical to satisfy the rigor metric. Can you try a second draft that's a lot more biting?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a revised set of critical comments with a more pointed and assertive tone:\n\nThe sweeping claim that SwiftAttention maintains long-range dependencies through its sliding window approach is underpinned by a flawed experimental design. Specifically, the use of a local diagnosis metric (perplexity) on GLUE benchmarks lacks the nuance required to meaningfully assess the model's ability to generalize to tasks involving extensive contextual relationships. This superficial evaluation fails to account for the inherent limitations of the sliding window approach in capturing global dependencies, and therefore, it is misleading to imply that SwiftAttention effectively retains this capacity.\n\nFurthermore, the Global Token Set employed by the authors does not provide a convincing mechanism for why its use in conjunction with the sliding window approach would yield improved long-range dependency capture. In fact, the mere assertion that this setup reduces quadratic complexity dilutes by unaddressed concerns regarding the impact of context boundaries on model outputs.\n\nFurthermore, it is striking that the authors' sparse-masking technique is presented as a panacea for reducing quadratic complexity, yet they fail to disclose the implicit trade-offs involved in skipping over contextual information. This approach unwittingly undermines the validity of the results, as ignoring one half of the problem only excises symptoms, rather than undertakes comprehensive",
      "source": "target",
